VSL Mixer
=========

A native, low-latency Mac mixer for the PreSonus AudioBox 1818VSL.
PreSonus's VSL software, which did the box's mixing, no longer runs on
current macOS, and the box has no onboard DSP, so VSLMixer/ replaces it:
it talks to the AudioBox directly through CoreAudio.

- Eight inputs, plus a ninth (Keys) when a USB keyboard's audio is
  plugged in, each with high-pass, 3-band EQ and a compressor.
- Mixed to Main and mono or stereo auxes on outs 1-8, with per-input pan
  on stereo pairs for in-ear monitors, a limiter on every output, and
  per-output delay.
- Raw multitrack recording.
- Musicians control their own aux from their phones' browsers, and the
  whole board can be run from a phone with a passcode.
- A feedback screen showing every input's spectrum.
- Can take inputs 1-2 from Jammer running alongside it.

See VSLMixer/README.md for background, measurements, design, building
and testing.


History: the 2012 version (obsolete)
------------------------------------

This repository started in 2012 as a small web remote for PreSonus's own
VSL app, not a mixer.  vsl1818.py ran on the same computer as VSL, spoke
VSL's remote-control protocol, and served a page at http://localhost:8000
for adjusting levels (master, aux3/4, aux5/6, aux7/8), panning and the
high-pass filter for every channel, from a browser or phone.  It was
updated through early 2014.  Since VSL itself no longer runs, it no
longer works and is kept only for history, along with the files that
went with it:

  vsl1818.py                  the remote (Python 2)
  process_full_sweep.py       scratch code for mapping VSL's gain/pan to
                              separate left/right levels
  full_sweep__p_g_l_r.log,
  translated_full_sweep.csv   the measurements behind that mapping

Demo video: http://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ciWhHaIoN0A
Details and background: http://www.jefftk.com/news/2012-12-21
